{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was a key figure in the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140. He spent much of his early life in Antioch where his mother Hodierna was from. In 1152 his father was killed by the Hashashin which left Raymond as the heir to Tripoli. Since he was only a child his mother acted as regent until he reached adulthood. Raymond III took full control of Tripoli in 1155. His early reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim states surrounding Tripoli particularly Nur ad-Din Zengi who sought to expand his influence in the region.\n\n**Conflict with Nur ad-Din and Involvement in Jerusalem**\n\nRaymond III faced constant pressure from Nur ad-Din who captured several fortresses near Tripoli. In 1164 Nur ad-Din defeated a combined force of Raymond III and Bohemond III of Antioch at the Battle of Harim. Raymond III was captured and held prisoner until 1172. After his release he played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He became a close ally of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and later supported Baldwin IV against factions that sought to undermine the king’s authority. Raymond III was also involved in the succession crisis after Amalric I’s death in 1174 supporting Baldwin IV against Miles of Plancy and later against Guy of Lusignan.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Marriage and Legacy**\n\nIn 1174 Raymond III married Eschiva of Bures who was the heiress of the Lordship of Galilee. This marriage strengthened his ties to the Kingdom of Jerusalem and increased his political influence. Raymond III was known for his diplomatic skills and his ability to navigate the complex alliances of the Crusader states. He played a crucial role in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he helped Baldwin IV defeat Saladin. Raymond III also mediated disputes between different Crusader factions and was a key figure in the regency government during Baldwin IV’s minority. His leadership was instrumental in maintaining the stability of the region during a turbulent period.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\n**Death and Succession**\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 without a male heir. His death marked the end of the original Tripoli line of Raymond of Saint-Gilles. He was succeeded by his cousin Raymond IV of Tripoli who was also known as Raymond of Toulouse. Raymond III’s death came at a critical time as Saladin had just unified much of the Muslim world and was preparing to launch a major offensive against the Crusader states. His legacy lived on through his support for Baldwin IV and his efforts to maintain the fragile unity of the Crusader states during his lifetime.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.22343038022518158}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.24694620072841644}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.77468371274881e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.02098255231976509}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0001006075763143599}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.172316494077677e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.3554084599018097}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.05948348715901375}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -6.318072337307967e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0002817710628733039}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.24226881563663483}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.14388447999954224}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.172316494077677e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.00092225451953709